From: VultaV <unc1010@gmail.com>
Subject: wifi: cfg80211/mac80211: set the TX power of one radio of a vif

On a multi-radio wiphy, a SET_WIPHY request with a radio index went to
nl80211_set_wiphy_radio(), which handles only the RTS threshold and
returns, so a TX power setting with a radio index was dropped while the
request still succeeded. Pass such a setting on with its radio index.

The per-vif path in mac80211 then applied the level to every link of
the interface. Apply it only to the links whose channel is on the given
radio, so one link of an AP MLD can be attenuated on its own.

Signed-off-by: VultaV <unc1010@gmail.com>
---
--- a/net/wireless/nl80211.c
+++ b/net/wireless/nl80211.c
@@ -4315,7 +4315,9 @@
 		if (radio_idx >= rdev->wiphy.n_radio)
 			return -EINVAL;
 
-		return nl80211_set_wiphy_radio(info, rdev, radio_idx);
+		/* A TX power setting takes the radio index to the driver below */
+		if (!info->attrs[NL80211_ATTR_WIPHY_TX_POWER_SETTING])
+			return nl80211_set_wiphy_radio(info, rdev, radio_idx);
 	}
 
 	if (info->attrs[NL80211_ATTR_WIPHY_TXQ_PARAMS]) {
--- a/net/mac80211/cfg.c
+++ b/net/mac80211/cfg.c
@@ -3767,6 +3767,12 @@
 			if (!link)
 				continue;
 
+			/* A radio index limits the setting to the links on it */
+			if (radio_idx >= 0 &&
+			    cfg80211_get_radio_idx_by_chan(wiphy,
+					link->conf->chanreq.oper.chan) != radio_idx)
+				continue;
+
 			link->user_power_level = user_power_level;
 
 			if (txp_type != link->conf->txpower_type) {
